\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400783} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d82c4fc-397c-5ccb-b692-70512b0dcfc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ad69a69-e154-5068-a0be-7ed17edd11f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-139603" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551340 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa5bf185-de29-5185-ba57-c64250332dfa" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Elements of a Polynomial Regression Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "625975e6-7869-5a67-bc59-519fa2d8371d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550604} {:answer/id #uuid "65dd863d-70c0-5170-aa9a-00b0ab5e1a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(-5)^{2} \\\\ (-25)^{2} \\\\ {5}^{2} \\\\ {20}^{2} \\\\ {35}^{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550618} {:answer/id #uuid "a348d2b4-ce93-5a99-a6ed-1906d407475a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(-3)^{2} \\\\ (-1)^{2} \\\\ {1}^{2} \\\\ {3}^{2} \\\\ {5}^{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550791} {:answer/id #uuid "a61433c9-2b22-57c8-9478-14e323e7d6b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -25 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 35\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550804} {:answer/id #uuid "a6559ef5-d440-5051-9b33-5e541b8f53b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a61433c9-2b22-57c8-9478-14e323e7d6b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -25 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 35\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550804} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4aab56a-66ce-5d92-bf05